 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Advance Directive Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is an advance directive form?\", \"a\": \"It is a legal document that records the medical care you want if you cannot speak for yourself, and names the person who decides for you. Use the version for your state, since the signing rules differ.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I create an advance directive form?\", \"a\": \"Start from your state's official form and turn each treatment it lists into a separate question in plain words. Hospitals, law firms, and care communities collecting many of them can describe the form to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>, which builds the questions and saves each answer to a database.\"}, {\"q\": \"Where can I get a blank advance directive form?\", \"a\": \"Your state health department or attorney general's office usually posts the official version. Hospitals and nursing homes keep blank copies on hand, and staff there can often help you fill one in.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a living will form used for?\", \"a\": \"A living will form records which treatments you want or refuse if you can no longer tell your doctors, such as CPR, a breathing machine, tube feeding, or dialysis. It puts your wishes in writing. The health care agent named in your directive is the person who acts on them.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the difference between a healthcare directive form and an advance directive?\", \"a\": \"Mostly the name. Minnesota uses health care directive for a document holding treatment instructions, a health care power of attorney, or both, which most states call an advance directive. Whatever the title, follow the form and signing rules of the state where you live.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I use a free advance directive form without a lawyer?\", \"a\": \"Yes. You do not need a lawyer to complete an advance directive, and the form your state posts costs nothing to use. Legal help is worth paying for when your wishes are unusual or your family disagrees. For clinics handing the form to many patients, Emergent's free tier covers building and publishing a guided version with a live URL.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a simple advance directive form include?\", \"a\": \"Four parts cover most needs. Name the health care agent and one alternate, then record your choices on named treatments such as CPR and tube feeding. Add a few lines on what comfort means to you, and finish with the witness or notary block your state requires. On a form built with Emergent, each part can sit on its own screen, one question at a time.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are examples of choices on an advance directive form?\", \"a\": \"Typical entries look like this. No CPR if my heart stops and recovery is unlikely. A breathing machine for a short trial only. No tube feeding if I am permanently unconscious. Full pain relief even if it makes me drowsy. Each one names a treatment and the situation it covers.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is required on a California advance directive form?\", \"a\": \"A notary's acknowledgment or the signatures of two adult witnesses. Your health care agent and your doctor or their staff cannot witness, and one of the two witnesses must have no family tie to you and no claim on your estate. Law firms can catch a conflict early with a legal intake form that asks who will witness.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the Texas advance directive form called?\", \"a\": \"Texas has no single form. Most people mean the Directive to Physicians and Family or Surrogates, for treatment wishes, or the Medical Power of Attorney, naming your agent. The state also posts an out-of-hospital DNR order. A notary can replace the witnesses on either main form if the law's conditions are met. Clinics can ask which one a patient has on their patient intake form.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
